From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude Agent <claude-agent@invalid>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 16:56:23 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] backends/color: Validate EDID primaries before using them for
 native SDR

In the native SDR color mode the output color state takes its
colorimetry from the EDID's default color primaries, unchecked. Nothing
guarantees those form a color space: zero, collinear or otherwise
degenerate chromaticities (or a white point with y = 0) produced a color
state for which ClutterColorTransform cannot build its matrices, and
until now that rendered with an identity matrix in place of the
display's gamut. This was the remaining producer of parametric color
states that bypassed validation.

Add meta_color_colorimetry_from_edid_primaries(), which accepts the
EDID's primaries only if they are declared, can be published exactly
(meta_color_primaries_to_fixed()) and pass
clutter_colorimetry_get_linear_bt2020_matrices(), the function the color
transform itself uses. When they are unusable, the native SDR mode keeps
the colorimetry of the default mode (sRGB) and the EDID-derived EOTF,
and logs a warning naming the connector once per color device. A
missing EDID now takes the same path instead of being dereferenced.

The function is exported for tests.

Tests:
 - /native-hdr/sdr-native-edid-primaries: usable primaries are taken
   as-is; collinear primaries, white y = 0, a white point outside the
   primaries, undeclared primaries and a missing EDID are rejected and
   leave the colorimetry untouched.
 - /wayland/sdr-native/unusable-edid-primaries: with collinear EDID
   primaries in native SDR mode, the output color state uses sRGB with
   the EDID's gamma 2.2 and the warning is logged exactly once across
   repeated updates. It rewrites the fixture output, so it runs as a
   separate invocation.
